          "message": {
            "text": "os.system or os.popen executes a dynamically constructed command through the shell. Review the command's trust boundary. Remediation: Use subprocess with a fixed executable and argument list, shell=False, and explicit allowed options. Agent/MCP relevance: An MCP command tool may interpolate planner output into os.system or os.popen. Those APIs introduce a shell boundary that can expand attacker-controlled syntax into the server account privileges. Fix steps: 1. Replace the operation with a native library call or subprocess.run([fixed_executable, validated_argument], shell=False, timeout=...). Split intentional pipelines into explicit stages with bounded output. Verify: Use a harmless fixture containing shell metacharacters and assert it is processed as one argument or rejected, without a second command. 2. Bind the tool name to a fixed executable and authorized workspace; validate flags and resource ownership independently of the prompt and of shell escaping. Verify: Call the server directly with an unauthorized path, extra option and different executable; verify rejection even without the agent planner. 3. Supply a minimal environment, avoid carrying gateway/cloud credentials into the child, and enforce cancellation plus output limits. Verify: Inspect the test child environment and attempt an overlong operation; confirm sensitive variables are absent and the process is stopped."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "agentMcpScan/v1": "0fa7e450cd583d78646ef397"
          },
          "properties": {
            "agentMcpRemediation": {
