          "message": {
            "text": "Certificate verification is explicitly disabled. A network intermediary may impersonate the remote service and capture prompts, credentials, or tool results. Remediation: Enable certificate verification and install the correct CA bundle; use a trusted private CA for custom gateways. Agent/MCP relevance: The affected connection can carry model credentials, prompts, MCP tool requests or retrieved evidence. Disabling TLS verification lets a reachable intermediary impersonate that peer and influence both data and agent decisions. Fix steps: 1. Remove verify=False, CERT_NONE and hostname-check bypasses. Use the client default trust store or supply an approved CA bundle through its supported verification setting. Verify: Against a test endpoint, reject an untrusted certificate and wrong hostname while accepting the intended certificate chain. 2. Distribute the private CA through the runtime trust configuration, verify the configured gateway hostname, and document CA rotation. Keep credentials out of URL query strings and diagnostics. Verify: Exercise certificate renewal and CA rollover in a staging environment; confirm the client never retries with verification disabled. 3. Check environment variables, shared HTTP sessions, proxies and image/deployment configuration for remaining bypasses on model and MCP clients. Verify: Run the actual deployment client through the intended proxy/gateway and retain handshake results plus redacted configuration evidence."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "agentMcpScan/v1": "07fc5f6fd7699fd0194dc479"
          },
          "properties": {
            "agentMcpRemediation": {
              "agent_mcp_relevance": "The affected connection can carry model credentials, prompts, MCP tool requests or retrieved evidence. Disabling TLS verification lets a reachable intermediary impersonate that peer and influence both data and agent decisions.",
              "applicability": [
                "Confirm which client and effective session settings apply; the detected literal does not prove this connection is used in production.",
                "A private gateway CA is a trust-distribution requirement, not a reason to disable peer verification."
              ],
